Below is our step‐by‐step reasoning and the resulting goal model decomposition for the described multi‐robot resource delivery mission.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 1. Understand the Mission  
─────────────────────────────  
• The mission has two major phases:  
 – Collection Phase: The robot must navigate to storage, request the resource (with precise specifications), wait for retrieval, and if its battery falls below 10% during this phase, it must return to the recharging station and reassign the mission.  
 – Delivery Phase: After retrieval, the robot (or robots) must deliver the resource(s) to a specified destination by making as many runs as needed. If the battery drops below 30% during delivery, the robot must return the resource to a checkpoint and reassign the remaining task; if that fails, an alert is raised and a report sent to the sector manager.  
• Additionally, when multiple items are needed (from different storages), multiple robots can work in parallel on collect–deliver tasks to reduce overall mission time.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 2. Identify Major High-Level Goals  
─────────────────────────────  
We propose a top‐level goal (G1) “Complete Resource Delivery Mission” whose fulfillment can occur in one of two ways:  
 – Method 1 (sequential): A single robot executes the collection phase (G2) followed by the delivery phase (G3).  
 – Method 2 (alternative): Multiple robots operate in parallel (G4) when multiple items are required.  
Since either approach completes the mission, we represent this as an OR decomposition between the two methods. We use a “FALLBACK” operator in our runtime annotation to indicate that if the sequential method is not viable (for example, due to battery conditions), the parallel alternative can be used.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 3. Decompose Collection (G2) and Delivery (G3)  
─────────────────────────────  
For the Collection phase (G2), we include:  
 • G2.1: Navigate to Storage  
 • G2.2: Request Resource  
 • G2.3: Retrieve Resource  
 • G2.4: Recharge and Reassign (fallback if battery < 10%)  
Because all these steps (except the fallback) must be executed sequentially, we use a sequential (“;”) operator and then a fallback option for low battery.

For the Delivery phase (G3), we include:  
 • G3.1: Plan Delivery Route  
 • G3.2: Deliver Resource  
 • G3.3: Handle Low Battery in Delivery (fallback if battery < 30% and, if that fails, trigger an alert)  
Again, these are executed sequentially with a fallback alternative.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 4. Define the Parallel Option (G4)  
─────────────────────────────  
G4 represents the “Parallel Collect–Deliver Tasks” goal. In this alternative, when multiple resources from different storages are needed, several robots can be assigned concurrently to perform navigation, collection, and delivery. Since this is a high-level alternative method (and for simplicity we treat it as a leaf goal in the decomposition), its runtime annotation is “-” (indicating no further sub‐goals), and its associated tasks (AT8 and AT9) are executed in parallel.

─────────────────────────────  
Step 5. Associate Tasks with Goals  
─────────────────────────────  
For each goal sub-component, we define concrete tasks (with an associated location and number of robots) that a robot (or group of robots) can immediately execute. For example:  
 – Task AT1 (“Navigate to Storage”) is assigned to G2.1.  
 – Task AT2 (“Send Resource Request”) is assigned to G2.2.  
 – Task AT3 (“Retrieve Resource”) is assigned to G2.3.  
 – Task AT4 (“Recharge and Reassign”) corresponds to G2.4.  
 – Tasks AT5 and AT6 correspond to G3.1 and G3.2 respectively.  
 – Task AT7 corresponds to G3.3.  
 – For the parallel approach (G4), tasks AT8 (“Parallel Navigation and Collection”) and AT9 (“Parallel Delivery Execution”) are defined.

